Data Structures Assignment 
Array, Stack, and Queue — Menu-Driven Programs 
Assignment Policy 
 This assignment has been intentionally kept simple so that every student is able to complete it 
and can focus on genuinely understanding data structures rather than struggling with 
complexity. 
 If you get stuck, you are encouraged to seek help from your course slides, classmates, or the 
internet — but strictly for the purpose of understanding concepts, not for obtaining ready-made 
solutions. 
 The use of AI tools to generate solutions, or the copying of assignments from any source, is 
strictly prohibited. All submitted work must be your own. 
 Your viva will conduct in your first lab class. 
General Instructions 
 Implement all programs in Java. 
 Solve all 5 questions (Array, Stack, Queue, Real-World Examples, and 
Advantages/Disadvantages). 
 Use a single array/variable to store your data structure (no built-in library structures like 
ArrayList, Stack class, Queue class, list.append()-based dynamic tricks, etc. — 
implement the logic yourself). 
 Each program must be menu-driven (a loop that keeps showing options until the user 
chooses Exit). 
 You may implement each solution either using a class (OOP style, with the array and 
size/top/front/rear as class members) or without a class (plain functions/procedures 
operating on a global or passed-in array). Choose whichever style your course requires, 
or do both for practice. 
 Assume a fixed maximum size for the array/stack/queue (e.g., 10 or 20), decided by you 
at the start of the program. 
 You can use int data tpe 
 Add simple validation (e.g., check for overflow, underflow, and invalid index) and print 
clear messages for each operation. 
Question 1 — Array Operations 
Scenario: A small library wants a simple program to manage the list of book IDs currently on a 
shelf. The shelf can hold a limited number of books at a time. The librarian needs a menu to 
manage this list from the console.  
Also write advantages and disadvantages of array data structure. 
Requirements: Implement an Array-based program that supports the following operations 
through a menu: 
1. Add a new value to the end of the array 
2. Insert a value at a specific index (given by the user), shifting existing elements to make 
room 
3. Fill the array with values (read multiple values from the user until the array is full or the 
user stops) 
4. Delete the last element from the array 
5. Delete by index (given by the user), shifting remaining elements to fill the gap 
6. Display all elements currently in the array 
7. Search for a value (return whether it exists and at which index) 
8. Get the value at a specific index (given by the user) 
9. Replace / Update the value at a specific index with a new value given by the user 
10. Size show the current number of elements in the array 
11. Exit 
Sample Menu: ----- ARRAY MENU ----- 
1. Add value 
2. Insert at index 
3. Fill array 
4. Delete last element 
5. Delete by index 
6. Display 
7. Search value 
8. Get value at index 
9. Replace/Update value at index 
10. size 
11. Exit 
Enter your choice: 
Question 2 — Stack Operations 
Scenario: You have a pile of plates (whole numbers written on each plate) stacked one on top of another. 
You can only add a new plate on top, and you can only remove the plate that is currently on top. Build a 
program to simulate this stack of integer values. 
Requirements: Implement a Stack-based program (using a single array and a top variable) that 
supports the following operations through a menu: 
1. Push — add a new action/value to the top of the stack 
2. Pop — remove and show the value from the top of the stack 
3. Display — show all values currently in the stack (top to bottom or bottom to top) 
4. Size — show the current number of elements in the stack 
5. isEmpty — check whether the stack is empty 
6. isFull — check whether the stack has reached its maximum capacity 
7. Exit 
Sample Menu: ----- STACK MENU ----- 
1. Push 
2. Pop 
3. Display 
4. Size 
5. isEmpty 
6. isFull 
7. Exit 
Enter your choice: 
Question 3 — Queue Operations 
Scenario: A small bank counter serves customers using token numbers (integers). Every arriving 
customer is given a token number, which joins the back of the queue, and the counter always serves the 
token at the front of the queue first. Build a program to manage this queue of integer token numbers. 
Requirements: Implement a Queue-based program (using a single array and front/rear 
variables) that supports the following operations through a menu: 
1. Enqueue — add a new print job/value to the back of the queue 
2. Dequeue — remove and show the value from the front of the queue 
3. Display — show all values currently in the queue from front to rear 
4. Size — show the current number of elements in the queue 
5. isEmpty — check whether the queue is empty 
6. isFull — check whether the queue has reached its maximum capacity 
7. Exit 
Sample Menu: ----- QUEUE MENU ----- 
1. Enqueue 
2. Dequeue 
3. Display 
4. Size 
5. isEmpty 
6. isFull 
7. Exit 
Enter your choice: 
Question 4 — Real-World Examples  
Task: For each data structure write, 5 real-world (or software) examples where that data 
structure is a natural fit. For every example, write one line explaining why that data structure 
suits the scenario. Also write their advantages and disadvantages. 
